RFC
An RFC (Request for Comments) is more than just a design document—it is a collaborative proposal that must inform, persuade, and invite constructive feedback.

Here's a comprehensive breakdown of what to include and why each element matters.

Title
Be descriptive and specific (e.g., "RFC: Unified Logging Format for Microservices")
RFC ID / Number (Optional)
Helps in tracking and referencing in conversations,
Author(s)
Clarifies accountability and the right person to contact for questions.
Status
Draft | In Review | Approved | Rejected | Implemented | Superseded
Created / Last Updated
Adds temporal context—especially helpful for reviews and prioritization.
Summary / Abstract
What is being proposed?
Why is it important now?
Who/what is affected?
Motivation / Problem Statement
The current state and its limitations.
User or stakeholder needs.
Proposed Solution / Design
Overview of the solution (code, service, or feature).
System/component diagrams.
Interface changes (API, CLI, UX).
Data models or schema updates.
Code example snippets.
Security and privacy considerations.
Alternatives Considered
Impact
Open Questions / Feedback Sought
Shows humility and invites specific feedback.
"Should we X or Y?"
Areas needing domain expert input
​​References / Appendix


